Both annotations can be used to test a controller, but they serve different purposes and have very different costs.

@WebMvcTest is the focused choice. It loads only the web layer — your controllers, filters, exception handlers, and MockMvc. Services and repositories are not loaded, so you mock them with @MockBean. This test runs fast because there's minimal context to create. You use it when you want to test: that the URL maps correctly to the controller method, that request parameters or body are correctly deserialized, that the response JSON format is correct, and that error handling returns the right HTTP status codes.

@SpringBootTest loads the full application context: controllers, services, repositories, database — everything. This is the right choice when you want to test the complete request flow from HTTP through the controller, through the service, all the way to the database and back. It's slower but gives you end-to-end confidence.

The practical rule for most fresher projects: use @WebMvcTest to test controller behavior in isolation — verify your API contract. Use @SpringBootTest when you need to verify a complete use case that spans multiple layers.

A common interview answer is to say you'd use @WebMvcTest for 'unit testing the controller' and @SpringBootTest for 'integration testing the whole stack.' This shows you understand the speed vs confidence trade-off.
